  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › Language exchange</p>
  <h1>What is language exchange?</h1>
  <p class="lede">Language exchange is a simple idea: two people who speak different languages meet — in person or online — and help each other learn. You spend half the time speaking your partner’s language and half speaking yours. It costs nothing, and it gives you real conversation practice with a real person.</p>

  <div class="ad-slot" role="complementary" aria-label="Advertisement"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

  <h2>How language exchange works</h2>
  <p>A typical language exchange works like this. You find a partner who speaks the language you are learning and wants to learn yours — for example, you speak English and want to learn Spanish, and your partner speaks Spanish and wants to learn English. You agree a time, usually 30–60 minutes, and split it in half: 30 minutes in Spanish, 30 minutes in English.</p>
  <p>You can meet in a café, on a video call, or through a language exchange app or website. Some people meet the same partner every week for months; others try a new partner each time. The best exchanges have a simple plan for each meeting: a topic to talk about, and an agreement to correct each other’s mistakes.</p>
  <h2>The honest pros</h2>
  <p>The biggest advantage is that it is free, and it gives you real conversation with a native speaker — something no app can fully replace. You hear natural, everyday language, you learn slang and culture, and you often make a friend. For many learners, a language exchange partner is the first real conversation they ever have in their new language, and that is a huge confidence boost.</p>
  <p>It is also flexible: you choose when and how often to meet, and you can talk about anything that interests you both — films, food, travel, work — which keeps it enjoyable in a way that textbooks often are not.</p>
  <h2>The honest cons</h2>
  <p>Language exchange has real downsides, and it is fair to name them. First, your partner is not a teacher: they may not know how to explain grammar, and they may not correct your mistakes — or may correct them too much. Second, the time is often unequal: one person talks more, or one language dominates the session.</p>
  <p>Third, there is no structure and no plan. You might chat happily for an hour and learn very little. And fourth, finding a reliable partner takes time: people cancel, disappear, or only want to practise one language. If you need steady, structured progress — for an exam, a job, or a deadline — language exchange alone is rarely enough.</p>
  <h2>How to make language exchange work well</h2>
  <p>Set clear rules from the first meeting. Agree the time split in advance — for example, 30 minutes in each language — and use a timer if you need to. Agree how you want to be corrected: some people want every mistake fixed, others want only the big ones. Pick a topic before each meeting, so you are not staring at each other wondering what to say.</p>
  <p>Prepare a little before each session: five new words you want to use, or three questions to ask. After the session, write down the words and corrections you learned. And be a good partner yourself: correct your partner kindly, stick to the time split, and show up on time.</p>
  <h2>Language exchange vs 1-to-1 tutoring</h2>
  <p>Language exchange and tutoring are not the same thing, and they suit different needs. Language exchange is free, social and good for practising conversation you already have the basics for. Tutoring costs money, but it gives you a trained teacher, a plan, structured correction and lessons built around your goal.</p>
  <p>Many learners use both: a tutor for structured learning and a language exchange partner for extra speaking practice. If you are a complete beginner, start with a tutor or a course first — language exchange works best once you can already hold a simple conversation.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Treating it like a free lesson and expecting your partner to teach you — they are a partner, not a teacher.</li>
    <li>Letting one language take over the whole session — agree the split and stick to it.</li>
    <li>Never correcting each other — without corrections, mistakes become habits.</li>
    <li>Meeting without a topic and just chatting — fun, but you will learn slowly.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Agree the time split and the correction rules in your first meeting.</li>
    <li>Prepare five words or three questions before each session.</li>
    <li>Write down corrections and new words after every session.</li>
    <li>Use both: a tutor for structure, a language exchange partner for extra practice.</li>
  </ul>

