  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>Is language exchange free?</summary><p>Usually, yes. Most language exchange partners meet for free, because both people benefit — each one gets conversation practice in the language they are learning. Some apps and websites charge for extra features, but finding a partner and meeting them is normally free.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is language exchange enough to learn a language?</summary><p>For conversation practice, it is excellent. For learning a language from zero, it is usually not enough on its own — you also need structured learning for grammar, vocabulary and pronunciation. Most successful learners combine language exchange with lessons, a course, or a tutor.</p></details>
  <details><summary>How do I find a language exchange partner?</summary><p>Through language exchange apps and websites, local language meetups, university language societies, or social media groups for learners of your language. Look for someone at a similar level who is reliable and wants to meet regularly — a regular partner is worth far more than many one-off chats.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is language exchange safe?</summary><p>Meet in public places or on video calls, keep your personal details private at first, and trust your instincts — if someone makes you uncomfortable, end the conversation. Reputable language exchange platforms have reporting and blocking tools; use them if you need to.</p></details>
